    "ExplanationWrongA": "Option A celebrates the net $87,000 favorable variance as evidence of strong cost management and recommends sharing it with the board. This analysis ignores the critical tradeoff: the $42,000 materials savings and $28,000 labor overrun are causally linked — the savings in one category drove additional costs in another. Treating them as independent wins and losses overstates the true cost improvement by $28,000. More importantly, presenting the net favorable variance to the board as 'effective cost control' when $28,000 of the labor overrun represents a process problem may create complacency about a purchasing decision that needs re-evaluation. Under CMA Part 1, variance reporting should decompose the total into its components and identify tradeoffs, not present the net as a summary judgment. A candidate selecting this option may have focused on the aggregate outcome rather than the causal structure producing it.",
