    "ExplanationCorrect": "The Controller's decomposition of the $154,000 static budget variance into $176,000 unfavorable volume variance and $22,000 favorable flexible budget variance provides the most accurate and actionable interpretation. The sales director is correct that the volume decline is the dominant driver — but the $22,000 favorable flexible budget variance demonstrates that management controlled costs effectively at the achieved volume, preventing the $154,000 miss from being $176,000. This decomposition matters for three reasons: (1) It separates what management can control (cost efficiency at a given volume, measured by the flexible budget variance) from what is market-driven (volume, measured by the sales volume variance); (2) It prevents the board from attributing the entire $154,000 to poor management when $22,000 of cost management performance was actually favorable; and (3) It directs corrective action to the right department — the sales team for volume recovery, not the operations team for cost cutting. Under CMA Part 1, the flexible budget is specifically designed to separate volume effects from cost-efficiency effects so that managers are evaluated on what they control. A common exam trap is to present the static budget variance alone as a summary performance measure, obscuring the two different causal forces that produced it.",
