    "ExplanationCorrect": "The Controller should recommend retaining the flexible budget system. The three-year data provides compelling evidence: Year 2 demonstrates that managers can deliver substantial cost savings ($320,000) even when market conditions depress volume — denying them bonuses in that scenario would punish effective management and likely cause the most capable managers to seek employment at companies that evaluate them on controllable performance. Year 3 demonstrates the reverse problem: the static budget would award full bonuses for windfall volume gains that the managers did not create, while ignoring that they overspent relative to what the volume justified. Under CMA Part 1, the controllability principle holds that managers should be evaluated on factors they can influence — market volume is not controllable by production or purchasing managers, but cost efficiency at a given volume is. The flexible budget isolates controllable performance from uncontrollable market conditions. The CEO's argument that 'managers committed to the plan' confuses commitment to a forecast with accountability for the outcome of an uncontrollable variable — managers commit to achieving cost targets at the volume that materializes, not to making the volume itself materialize. A common exam trap is to conflate budget commitment (a planning tool) with performance accountability (an evaluation tool).",
