    "ExplanationWrongA": "",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "Option B proposes a compromise — static budget with a volume-adjustment modifier. This sounds reasonable but introduces a new set of problems. The modifier (e.g., +/-5% per 10% volume deviation) is arbitrary — why 5% and not 3% or 8%? The flexible budget already provides the theoretically correct adjustment by recalculating what costs should have been at the actual volume using the standard cost relationships built into the budget. Adding a discretionary modifier on top of a static budget essentially recreates a flexible budget through an ad hoc mechanism — but less precisely and with more opportunity for managerial gaming (managers will lobby for the modifier formula that benefits their division). Under CMA Part 1, when the theoretically correct tool (flexible budget) already exists, substituting an approximation with arbitrary parameters is not an improvement. A candidate selecting this option may have attempted to reconcile the two positions rather than evaluating which framework better serves the performance evaluation objective.",
