    "ItemStyle": "single-select",
    "Stem": "Fenwick Manufacturing's Controller, Diana Park, is presenting the annual capital expenditure budget to the board's finance committee. The company's primary production line uses a CNC machining center purchased eight years ago for $1,800,000. Maintenance costs have escalated from $45,000 three years ago to $112,000 last year, and unplanned downtime caused $285,000 in lost production and expedited shipping costs in the prior fiscal year. Diana's team evaluated three options: (1) Replace the CNC center immediately at $2,400,000, financed through a five-year equipment loan at 6.8%, projected to reduce annual maintenance to $18,000 and eliminate 90% of downtime losses — but the added debt would increase Fenwick's debt-to-equity ratio from 1.55:1 to approximately 1.82:1, approaching the 2.0:1 covenant with its primary lender; (2) Continue operating the existing CNC center with an $85,000 annual maintenance contract and a $60,000 investment in predictive-maintenance sensors, extending its useful life by an estimated three years at reduced reliability; or (3) Outsource the production volume to a contract manufacturer at 12% above Fenwick's current variable cost of $78 per unit (80,000 units annually), eliminating the capital outlay entirely but surrendering quality control and process innovation. The board is divided — two members consider the covenant risk unacceptable, while the operations director insists that further deferral will lead to a catastrophic failure. Which recommendation should Diana present to the finance committee?",
