    "Stem": "Ashvale Manufacturing's CFO, Raj Mehta, must decide how to acquire a new automated packaging line for the company's expanded distribution center. The equipment costs $620,000 and has a seven-year useful life with negligible salvage value. Straight-line depreciation would apply if purchased. Two financing options are available: (1) Purchase with a five-year equipment loan at 6.5%, requiring a $50,000 down payment and monthly payments of approximately $11,148 (total payments $668,880 including interest of $98,880), with Ashvale retaining ownership and the associated depreciation tax shield (combined federal and state tax rate 25%). Annual maintenance is estimated at $22,000 under ownership. (2) Lease the equipment through a seven-year operating lease at $8,200 per month ($98,400 annually), with the lessor responsible for all maintenance. The lease would keep the $50,000 down payment in Ashvale's working capital and would not increase reported debt on the balance sheet, preserving the company's 1.70:1 debt-to-equity ratio. However, Ashvale would forgo the depreciation tax shield and build no equity in the asset. The operations director argues strongly for ownership to avoid perpetual lease payments, while the treasurer wants to preserve working capital for a potential acquisition. Which recommendation should Raj present to the CEO?",
