    "ExplanationWrongA": "",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "Option 2 (leasing) has the lowest after-tax annual cost in Year 1 ($73,800 vs. approximately $98,000+ for ownership in Year 1 after netting the $50,000 down payment), and the working-capital preservation argument has genuine merit if the CEO confirms the acquisition is imminent. However, the lease commits Ashvale to $98,400 annually in perpetuity — after seven years, cumulative lease payments total $688,800 with zero equity in the equipment, and Ashvale must either continue leasing (another $98,400/year) or purchase new equipment at then-current prices. The ownership path costs more in early years but ceases major payments after Year 5 while providing two more years of depreciation tax shield. The net present value of ownership vs. leasing depends on the discount rate, but the breakeven typically occurs between Years 5-7 for equipment of this class. Additionally, the lease surrenders maintenance control — the lessor chooses when and how to maintain the equipment, which may not align with Ashvale's production schedule. A candidate selecting this option may have prioritized first-year cash flow and working-capital preservation over total cost of ownership and operational autonomy.",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "Option C correctly identifies that total cost of ownership favors purchase over a seven-year horizon — this is the right analytical framework but the wrong recommendation delivery. The calculation is imprecise: the comparison ignores the time value of money (lease payments are spread evenly while purchase costs are front-loaded), the residual value of the equipment after seven years (even negligible salvage has option value for trade-in or redeployment), and the operational risk of depending on a lessor for maintenance of mission-critical packaging equipment. More fundamentally, this option presents a calculation-based recommendation without addressing the stakeholder conflict — the treasurer's acquisition concern and the operations director's ownership argument are the decision context, not just the net cost. A complete recommendation must address both the quantitative case and the qualitative factors that will persuade both stakeholders. A candidate selecting this option may have done the cost comparison correctly but failed to present it in a way that resolves the organizational conflict driving the decision.",
    "ExplanationWrongD": "Option D proposes a middle-ground approach — a five-year lease with a purchase option. This superficially addresses both stakeholder concerns: working-capital preservation (treasurer) and eventual ownership (operations director). However, lease-with-purchase-option arrangements typically embed a premium in the lease rate (the lessor prices the option), meaning Ashvale would pay above-market rent for five years and then pay fair market value to buy — the worst of both worlds economically. If the acquisition proceeds, Ashvale may need the working capital but will have paid an option premium for five years. If the acquisition does not proceed, Ashvale will have paid a lease premium and still faces a buyout at market price. The recommendation substitutes a compromise structure for an analytical comparison — the correct approach is to evaluate whether ownership or leasing better serves Ashvale's long-term interests, not to split the difference. A candidate selecting this option may have prioritized conflict resolution over analytical rigor.",
