    "Stem": "Underhill Manufacturing's CFO, Marcus Chen, must present the three-year capital expenditure plan to the board of directors. The company currently operates with equipment averaging 14 years old — maintenance costs have increased 22% over the past two years and unplanned downtime caused $340,000 in lost production last year. Marcus's team prepared three scenarios: (1) replace all production equipment immediately at $4.2 million, financed through a term loan at 7.2%, projected to reduce maintenance costs by $280,000/year and eliminate 80% of downtime losses; (2) phase replacement over four years at $1.1 million/year, maintaining some older equipment through the transition but spreading the financing burden; or (3) continue with the current equipment and increase the maintenance budget by $95,000/year while adding a second maintenance shift. The board is divided — two directors argue that $4.2 million in new debt will pressure the debt-to-equity covenant (currently 1.8:1, covenant limit 2.0:1). Which recommendation should Marcus present?",
