    "ExplanationWrongA": "Option A (permanent-only staffing) ignores the quarterly demand fluctuation. Hiring permanent workers for Q2 peak demand and laying them off in Q3 creates severance costs, unemployment insurance premium increases, and significant morale damage. The apparent $18.00/hour savings versus temporary workers at $22.50 is offset by (1) severance of approximately 2 weeks' pay per laid-off worker (~$21,600), (2) rehiring and retraining costs in future peak quarters, and (3) reputational damage that makes future hiring more difficult and expensive. Under CMA Part 1, labor budget decisions must consider the full cost of workforce management, not just the hourly wage rate. A candidate selecting this option may have minimized the visible hourly cost without accounting for the hidden costs of workforce instability.",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "Option C proposes 181 overtime hours per employee — more than double the contractual cap of 80 hours. This creates a direct labor-law compliance violation and potential grievance exposure. Even if employees voluntarily accept the hours, the company violates the negotiated contract and potentially applicable labor regulations. The overtime premium ($205,200) also exceeds the combined cost of Option B ($305,820 total vs. $357,120 total for pure overtime). More critically, worker fatigue from 7,600 overtime hours concentrated across 42 people substantially increases the risk of quality defects and workplace injuries — costs not captured in the labor rate alone. A candidate selecting this option may have prioritized in-house production control at any cost, ignoring contractual constraints and hidden quality/safety costs.",
    "ExplanationWrongD": "Option D subcontracts all overflow production at $24.00/unit equivalent. For 4,240 hours / 2.0 hours per unit = 2,120 units, the subcontracting cost is $50,880 — lower than temporary workers ($56,700 + $7,200 = $63,900). However, this option cedes all production flexibility: if the subcontractor delivers late or below quality standards, Whitmore has no internal capacity to absorb the shortfall (permanent workers are fully utilized, and no overtime or temporary workers are authorized). The subcontractor's $24.00/unit rate may also increase if demand exceeds expectations. Additionally, transferring proprietary production processes to an external partner creates intellectual property and quality control risks. Under CMA Part 1, make-vs-buy decisions in the direct labor budget must consider not just unit cost but also quality control, schedule reliability, and strategic capability retention. A candidate selecting this option may have minimized the visible labor cost without evaluating the operational risks of depending entirely on an external partner for peak production.",
