    "ItemStyle": "single-select",
    "Stem": "Yewbrook Manufacturing's production budget calls for 95,000 units in the upcoming fiscal year, each requiring 1.8 direct labor hours. The plant operates a single day shift at $22.00/hour with capacity for 140,000 labor hours annually (approximately 70 full-time-equivalent workers). The remaining 31,000 hours (171,000 required - 140,000 capacity) can be covered through: (a) a second shift at time-and-a-third ($29.33/hour), with an estimated 8% productivity loss during the first three months as new workers are trained, effectively requiring 33,480 scheduled hours to produce 31,000 productive hours; or (b) overtime at time-and-a-half ($33.00/hour) spread across the existing day-shift workforce, with a contractual cap of 200 overtime hours per worker per year (maximum 14,000 overtime hours from 70 workers). The operations manager notes that second-shift training costs average $1,800 per new hire, and approximately 22 new workers would be needed. Which analysis of the direct labor budget is most accurate?",
    "Choices": {
      "A": "Overtime is the lower-cost option — 31,000 hours at $33.00/hour = $1,023,000, compared to second-shift cost of 33,480 hours at $29.33/hour ($982,000) + 22 × $1,800 training ($39,600) = $1,021,600, making the two options nearly cost-equivalent, but overtime avoids the productivity loss and training complexity of a second shift",
      "B": "Overtime at 31,000 hours is not feasible — the 70 day-shift workers can only provide 14,000 overtime hours at the 200-hour contractual cap, covering less than half the capacity gap; the second shift is the only option that meets the production requirement, at a total cost of approximately $1,021,600",
      "C": "The optimal solution is a combination: 14,000 overtime hours ($462,000) + 17,000 second-shift hours ($498,610 using 17,850 scheduled hours at 95.2% productivity) + 10 new second-shift workers ($18,000 training) = $978,610 total, which is $43,000 less than either pure overtime or pure second-shift",
      "D": "The direct labor budget should be $3,762,000 — 171,000 hours at $22.00/hour — because the standard labor rate should not include shift differentials or overtime premiums, which are variances to be reported and investigated separately rather than built into the budgeted cost"
    },
    "CorrectChoice": "B",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "The most accurate analysis recognizes that the overtime option is capacity-constrained. Seventy day-shift workers × 200 overtime hours each = 14,000 hours maximum. The capacity gap is 31,000 hours, so overtime alone can cover at most 45% of the needed hours. The second shift is the only feasible option to meet the full production requirement. Cost: 33,480 scheduled hours × $29.33 = $982,000 (approximately) + 22 new workers × $1,800 = $39,600 training = $1,021,600 total. Note: the 33,480 scheduled hours accounts for the 8% productivity loss during training — 31,000 productive hours needed / 0.92 effective productivity rate during ramp-up ≈ 33,696 hours, rounded. The analysis must separately consider feasibility and cost — Option A compares costs as if both options are feasible when only the second shift is. Under CMA Part 1, the direct labor budget must be grounded in operational constraints (contractual overtime caps, shift capacity, training requirements), not just rate comparisons. A common exam trap is to compare hourly rates without checking whether the lower-rate option is actually available at the required volume.",
