    "ExplanationWrongA": "Option A states that overtime is the lower-cost option and compares $1,023,000 (overtime) to $1,021,600 (second shift), finding them near-equivalent. The cost comparison is directionally correct but the analysis ignores the binding capacity constraint: 70 workers × 200 hours = 14,000 overtime hours maximum, not the 31,000 hours assumed in the $1,023,000 calculation. The overtime cost for 14,000 hours would be $462,000 — far less than the $1,021,600 second-shift cost, but leaving 17,000 uncovered hours. The choice presents both options as independently feasible when one is not. A candidate selecting this option may have performed the rate comparison correctly but failed to apply the capacity constraint.",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "Option C proposes a combination: 14,000 overtime + 17,000 second-shift. This is the only option that correctly identifies both the overtime capacity constraint and the marginal second-shift hours needed. However, the cost calculation ($978,610) relies on a 95.2% productivity assumption for the second shift that contradicts the scenario's stated 8% productivity loss during training. The scenario specifies that 33,480 scheduled hours are needed to produce 31,000 productive hours for a pure second-shift solution (92.6% productivity during ramp-up). Using 95.2% for the partial second-shift without justification is inconsistent. The analytical approach (check capacity constraints, then combine sources) is correct, but the specific cost estimate is inconsistent with the stated assumptions. A candidate selecting this option may have correctly identified the combination approach but applied inconsistent productivity assumptions.",
    "ExplanationWrongD": "Option D argues that shift differentials and overtime premiums should be excluded from the budget and reported as variances. This reflects a misunderstanding of the direct labor budget's purpose. The direct labor budget should forecast the actual expected labor cost, including premiums for overtime and shift differentials if those are part of the production plan. Budgeting at the base rate ($22.00) when the company knows it will incur premium rates would systematically understate labor cost by $1,091,600 ($3,762,000 budgeted vs. ~$1,021,600 actual for second shift). Reporting the premium as a 'variance' every period would make the budget useless for cash planning and would condition management to ignore a variance that is entirely predictable. Under CMA Part 1, the budget should reflect expected actual costs, not ideal costs. A candidate selecting this option may have confused standard costing (which isolates rate variances) with budgeting (which forecasts expected costs).",
