    "ItemStyle": "single-select",
    "Stem": "Zionsgate Aerospace has a contract to produce 32 specialized wing assemblies. The first unit required 500 direct labor hours. Engineering estimates an 80% cumulative average-time learning curve applies to the first 32 units. The direct labor rate is $45/hour. The production manager, Elena Vargas, must budget the total direct labor cost for the contract. She also needs to assess whether the learning curve assumption is reasonable — the 80% rate is based on a similar contract three years ago, but the current workforce has 40% new hires who did not work on the previous contract. An alternative estimate from a more conservative production engineer suggests a 90% learning curve due to the workforce inexperience and because 15% of the assemblies use new composite materials not covered by the historical learning data. Which analysis of the direct labor budget is most appropriate?",
    "Choices": {
      "A": "Budget using the 80% learning curve: cumulative average time per unit for 32 units = 500 × 32^(log 0.80 / log 2) = 500 × 32^(-0.3219) = 500 × 0.3277 = 163.85 hours average; total hours = 163.85 × 32 = 5,243 hours; total labor cost = 5,243 × $45 = $235,935 — this uses the historically validated learning rate and provides the most accurate cost estimate",
      "B": "The 80% learning curve likely overstates productivity improvement given the 40% new-hire workforce and the new composite materials — the more conservative 90% curve (500 × 32^(-0.1520) = 500 × 0.5909 = 295.5 hours average, total 9,454 hours, $425,430 cost) is a more realistic budget basis, and the difference between the two estimates ($189,495) represents the risk premium that should be communicated to the customer as a contingency",
      "C": "Neither learning curve is reliable because the assumptions have changed — the Controller should budget using the most recent actual data (first-unit time of 500 hours) and treat any productivity improvement as a favorable variance rather than embedding an uncertain learning-rate assumption into the committed budget",
      "D": "The appropriate approach is to budget using the 80% curve for the first 16 units (workforce still learning the process) and switch to a steady-state productivity assumption for units 17-32 (workforce has reached the flat portion of the learning curve), with the steady-state rate derived from the cumulative average of units 15-16 rather than continuing the logarithmic decline"
    },
    "CorrectChoice": "B",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "Elena should budget using the 90% learning curve. The 80% learning curve represents an aggressive productivity improvement (each doubling of cumulative output reduces average time by 20%) that was achieved by an experienced workforce on familiar materials. Three years later, with 40% new hires and 15% new composite materials, the conditions that produced the 80% rate no longer hold. Workforce inexperience directly reduces the learning rate because new workers learn slower than experienced ones — they make more errors, require more supervision, and take longer to reach proficiency. New materials introduce process uncertainty that further slows learning. The 90% learning curve (only 10% reduction per doubling) is a more realistic estimate under these changed conditions. The $189,495 cost difference between the two estimates is the value of recognizing that historical learning rates are not automatically portable to new contracts with different workforce and material conditions. Under CMA Part 1, the direct labor budget should be based on attainable standards — standards that reflect expected actual conditions, not historical best-performance rates. A common exam trap is to apply a historical learning rate to a new situation without adjusting for changed conditions.",
