    "ExplanationWrongA": "Option A applies the 80% learning curve without adjusting for changed conditions. The calculation is mechanically correct but the input assumption is not valid. The 40% new-hire rate means the workforce is not the same workforce that achieved the 80% rate three years ago — the learning curve captures organizational learning, not just individual learning, and organizational learning degrades when experienced workers leave. The 15% new composite materials introduce a process variable that the historical data does not cover. Budgeting at $235,935 when the realistic cost is likely $425,430 would produce a $189,495 unfavorable variance that is entirely predictable. Under CMA Part 1, the budget should be based on attainable current standards, not historical rates that no longer reflect operating conditions. A candidate selecting this option may have performed the learning-curve calculation correctly but failed to evaluate whether the input assumptions are still valid.",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "Option C rejects both learning curves and proposes budgeting at the first-unit time (500 hours × 32 units = 16,000 hours, $720,000). This treats productivity improvement as an unknown that should not be budgeted — a conservative approach that avoids unfavorable variances but at the cost of producing a budget that is almost certainly wrong. Learning curves are well-established empirically in aerospace manufacturing; budgeting as if no learning occurs would overstate labor cost by $294,570-$484,065 (depending on the learning rate chosen). More importantly, the overstated budget would inflate the contract bid, potentially causing Zionsgate to lose the contract to a competitor whose budget reflects realistic learning-curve assumptions. A candidate selecting this option may have confused conservatism in budgeting (using a cautious learning rate) with abandoning learning-curve estimation entirely.",
    "ExplanationWrongD": "Option D proposes a split approach: learning curve for units 1-16, steady-state for units 17-32. This is conceptually sophisticated — recognizing that the learning curve flattens — but operationally unsupported. The scenario provides no data on when the learning curve reaches steady state. At 32 units, the cumulative average is still declining under both the 80% and 90% assumptions — meaning the workforce is still learning, not at steady state. Switching to a steady-state assumption at an arbitrary unit number (16) would understate labor cost for units 17-32, producing an unfavorable variance. A candidate selecting this option may have correctly identified that learning curves flatten but applied the steady-state switch at an arbitrary point rather than where the data supports it.",
