    "Stem": "Ashfield Manufacturing's COO has proposed investing $1,200,000 in robotic welding cells to replace 18 of the company's 42 welding positions. Each robotic cell costs $200,000 (six cells needed), has a seven-year useful life with $30,000 annual maintenance per cell, and can weld 12,000 units annually per cell — matching the output of three welders. The current welders earn $28.50/hour ($59,280 annually each at 2,080 hours) with 28% benefits load ($75,878 fully loaded). The company's weighted average cost of capital is 8%, tax rate is 25%, and straight-line depreciation applies. Production volume is stable at 72,000 units annually. The union contract requires six months' notice before any workforce reduction and guarantees severance of 12 weeks' pay ($13,154 per affected welder). The CFO, Marcus Chen, must evaluate the proposal. However, the production manager argues that robotic cells cannot handle the 15% of welds requiring complex angles and repositioning — those would still need skilled welders — and the sales director notes that two major customers have indicated they value the 'craftsmanship' of hand-welded products and may reduce orders if production is fully automated. Which recommendation should Marcus present to the board?",
