    "ExplanationCorrect": "Model 1 (budget committee) directly addresses the root cause of last year's failure: the absence of cross-departmental review. When department heads publicly dispute budgets at the board presentation, the problem is not that finance lacked technical expertise — it is that departments never saw or agreed to each other's numbers before the board saw them. A budget committee composed of senior managers from all major functions provides the forum for departments to reconcile competing resource claims (e.g., sales wants more inventory than production can build, R&D wants funding that reduces short-term profits) BEFORE the budget reaches the board. Under CMA Part 1 budgeting, the budget committee serves as the coordination mechanism that aligns functional plans into a coherent organizational plan. The 3 additional weeks are an investment in budget quality — the alternative (repeating last year's board presentation with disputed numbers) has higher organizational cost. The evaluate-level skill is identifying that the governance model must solve the specific problem (lack of cross-functional coordination) rather than optimizing a secondary metric (process speed).",
