    "ItemStyle": "single-select",
    "Stem": "Kelso Industries' controller, Lisa Tran, is preparing the Q2 variance analysis package for the audit committee. In April, a key supplier declared force majeure after a natural disaster, causing raw material prices for Kelso's primary input to increase from $14.50/unit to $19.80/unit — a 36.6% increase. The purchasing director estimates the new price will persist for at least 9-12 months while alternative suppliers are qualified. The Q1-Q2 budget was based on the $14.50 price. Kelso consumes 12,000 units of this material per quarter. Lisa calculates that maintaining the original price standard for Q3-Q4 would produce a guaranteed $127,200 unfavorable material price variance ($5.30/unit x 12,000 units x 2 quarters) that is entirely attributable to the external supply shock. The VP of Operations argues the budget should not be revised — 'we need to show the board the real impact of this supply disruption.' The CFO wants the budget to reflect expected costs accurately for Q3-Q4 planning. Which recommendation should Lisa make?",
    "Choices": {
      "A": "Do not revise the budget — hold managers accountable to the original $14.50/unit standard. The $127,200 unfavorable variance will communicate the supply shock's impact to the board more effectively than a revised budget that hides the cost increase. Budget revisions undermine the discipline of comparing actual to original plan",
      "B": "Revise the budget for Q3-Q4 to reflect the $19.80/unit price — the $14.50 standard is no longer achievable, and continuing to use it will produce variances that provide zero diagnostic value (everyone knows why costs are higher). The original budget can still be preserved for year-over-year comparison, but the operating budget for the remainder of the year should reflect the best available cost information",
      "C": "Revise the budget to a blended rate of $17.15 — the midpoint between the old and new prices. This partially acknowledges the cost increase while preserving some of the original budget's cost discipline message",
      "D": "Create two variance reports: one against the original $14.50 budget (to show the board the $127,200 supply shock impact) and one against a revised $19.80 budget (to evaluate operational performance excluding the supply shock). Present both to the audit committee for a complete picture"
    },
    "CorrectChoice": "B",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "The budget should be revised for Q3-Q4 to reflect the $19.80/unit price. Under CMA Part 1 budgeting, a budget revision is appropriate when a significant, persistent change in underlying assumptions makes the original budget unreliable as a planning and performance evaluation tool. The key facts: the price increase is large (36.6%), it will persist for 9-12 months (not a one-month spike), and it is external (the purchasing department did not cause it). Continuing with the $14.50 standard for two more quarters serves no purpose: the $127,200 unfavorable variance is 100% explained by the supply shock — it provides zero information about purchasing performance, production efficiency, or cost control. The budget's purpose is to provide a meaningful benchmark — a benchmark known to be wrong fails this purpose. The evaluate-level skill is distinguishing between events that warrant budget revision (persistent, structural changes to the cost baseline) and events that should be explained through variance analysis (one-time or controllable deviations).",
