    "ExplanationWrongA": "Option A argues against revision on the grounds of 'budget discipline.' Under CMA Part 1, budget discipline means holding managers accountable for controllable factors — it does not mean refusing to update assumptions when conditions have demonstrably changed. Running two quarters of variance reports showing a mechanical $127,200 unfavorable variance teaches the organization nothing and may actually undermine the credibility of the budget process — if managers see that the budget is deliberately maintained at an unachievable standard, they will discount all budget variances as 'just the supply shock' and miss real operational issues. The board can be informed of the supply shock's impact through a one-time disclosure without embedding it in two quarters of meaningless variance reports. A candidate selecting this option may have confused budget rigidity with accountability.",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "Option C proposes an arbitrary blended rate of $17.15 — the simple average of $14.50 and $19.80. This rate has no basis in reality: the company will pay $19.80/unit, not $17.15. Using a fictional rate of $17.15 means the variance report will show an unfavorable variance of $2.65/unit on every purchase — creating a persistent, predictable variance that, like the unrevised budget, provides no diagnostic value. Budget standards should reflect expected actual costs, not aspirational compromises between old and new rates. A candidate selecting this option may have attempted to 'split the difference' without recognizing that budget assumptions must be grounded in reality, not negotiation.",
    "ExplanationWrongD": "Option D proposes dual variance reports — one against each standard. While this seems comprehensive, it doubles the reporting burden and creates confusion about which numbers represent 'the budget.' More importantly, running two parallel sets of variance reports signals that the organization cannot commit to a single plan — it undermines the budget as the authoritative financial benchmark. A single set of reports against a revised, realistic budget, supplemented by a clear disclosure of the supply shock's impact and the revision rationale, provides the same information more clearly and with less administrative overhead. A candidate selecting this option may have attempted to satisfy all audiences without recognizing that multiple 'official' budgets create ambiguity about which targets managers are accountable for.",
