    "ExplanationWrongA": "",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "Option B (supply-shock adjustment line) is the most common compromise proposal in budget-revision disputes and has genuine appeal — it isolates the external impact without triggering the EPS guidance revision that the sales director fears. However, this approach has two structural flaws. First, 'isolating' an $84,000 per quarter variance in a single line item sounds clean but in practice the steel cost flows through multiple accounts — raw materials inventory, work-in-process, cost of goods manufactured, and ultimately COGS. The adjustment line would need to be a composite of all affected accounts, and any allocation of the $84,000 across these accounts is inherently judgmental. Second, the approach preserves the original budget as the performance benchmark but this benchmark is now known to be unachievable — managers will be held to a $3.40 standard that the company knows it cannot meet, which undermines the motivational purpose of the budget. CMA Part 1 distinguishes between controllable and uncontrollable variances, but the remedy for a permanent structural change is revision, not variance isolation. A candidate selecting this option may have prioritized avoiding a difficult analyst conversation over maintaining the integrity of the performance evaluation system.",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "Option C (rolling forecast) is an elegant conceptual solution but is not appropriate as an emergency response to a mid-year shock. Transitioning to a rolling forecast model requires process redesign — defining forecast horizons, updating data feeds, training budget owners on new submission templates, and establishing the governance around forecast updates. Rolling out this transformation in Q2 while simultaneously managing the tariff impact on current operations is organizationally unrealistic. More importantly, switching to a rolling forecast mid-year does not resolve the immediate problem: whether the remaining three quarters should be planned at $3.40 or $4.80. The board and analysts still need an annual EPS number for the current fiscal year — a rolling forecast that disclaims an annual target does not satisfy external reporting expectations. A candidate selecting this option may have confused a process-improvement recommendation with a decision on the immediate budget question.",
    "ExplanationWrongD": "Option D ($4.60/lb, analysts' floor) appears more optimistic than the recommended $4.80 — it projects a lower steel cost and a smaller EPS hit. However, adopting the floor of the analyst range ($4.60) when spot prices are at $5.15 is wishful budgeting, not conservative planning. If steel stays at or above $5.15, Lockhaven will report an unfavorable variance against even the revised budget — defeating the purpose of revision. The $0.20 cushion in the recommended $4.80 rate represents prudent contingency, not excessive conservatism. Additionally, the revised EPS guidance of $2.70-$2.80 is narrower than necessary ($0.10 range vs. typical $0.15-$0.20 guidance range) and may set up another guidance revision if steel prices don't decline to analysts' projections. A candidate selecting this option may have attempted to minimize the reported impact rather than maximize the accuracy of the revised plan.",
