    "Stem": "Mapleton Outdoor Gear's annual budget projected 120,000 units sold at an average price of $185, generating $22,200,000 in revenue with a 32% gross margin. In April, the company's largest retail customer — representing 28% of budgeted sales — filed for Chapter 11 bankruptcy protection, canceling all outstanding orders and returning $940,000 in inventory already shipped but unpaid. CFO Patricia Owens must decide how to adjust the budget for the remaining eight months. Her team developed three scenarios: (1) Revise the full-year budget downward to reflect the lost customer ($6,216,000 in revenue reduction) and reduce production, materials purchasing, and direct labor budgets proportionally — this would require a 15% workforce reduction at the company's primary manufacturing facility; (2) Maintain the original budget but classify the customer loss as a 'discontinued operation' variance outside the operating budget, keeping the remaining 72% of the business on its original plan while absorbing the $940,000 write-off as a separate charge; or (3) Revise the sales forecast to $18,500,000 (assuming 100,000 units at $185 and targeting replacement of 30% of the lost volume through expanded e-commerce and two new retail partnerships), and adjust production budgets to match — preserving 85% of the workforce while accepting a lower margin mix from e-commerce sales. The production VP warns that a workforce reduction will cause the most experienced workers to leave first. Which recommendation should Patricia present to the board?",
