    "ExplanationWrongA": "Scenario 1 treats the customer bankruptcy as a permanent, irrevocable revenue loss requiring an immediate, proportionate cost reduction. This is not unreasonable on purely financial grounds — the $6.2 million in revenue is genuinely gone, and operating with 28% excess capacity is expensive. However, this recommendation has two critical flaws. First, it assumes the lost volume cannot be replaced through any channel — a premature assumption given that the underlying consumer demand for Mapleton's products still exists and is now served by a bankrupt retailer whose customers will seek alternative purchasing channels. Second, the workforce reduction is a one-way decision: laying off 15% of the manufacturing workforce will cause the most experienced and mobile workers to leave (as the production VP warned), permanently degrading production capability. If replacement volume materializes six months later, Mapleton will need to hire and train new workers at significant cost and quality risk. Under CMA Part 1, budget revision should preserve strategic flexibility while addressing the immediate financial impact — Scenario 1 prioritizes cost alignment over strategic optionality. A candidate selecting this option may have treated the budget as purely a cost-control tool rather than also a resource-allocation and capability-preservation tool.",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "Scenario 2 classifies the customer loss as a 'discontinued operation' variance — an accounting treatment, not a business decision. Labeling the $6.2 million revenue loss as a separate variance line changes nothing about the operational reality: Mapleton has 28% excess production capacity, materials purchase commitments based on 120,000 units, and a workforce sized for the original revenue base. The variance label explains the difference between budget and actual after the fact — it does not guide resource allocation for the remaining eight months. More importantly, classifying a customer bankruptcy as a 'discontinued operation' is itself questionable — discontinued operations under GAAP refer to the disposal of a component of an entity representing a separate major line of business or geographical area, not the loss of a single customer. Applying this label to a customer loss may mislead the board and external stakeholders about the nature of the event. A candidate selecting this option may have confused financial reporting with operational planning.",
