    "ExplanationWrongC": "Option C claims the Controller double-counted April-to-May collections. This reflects a misunderstanding of the cash collection budget methodology. Cash collections in a given month properly include both current-month sales collected in the current month AND prior-month sales collected in the current month — they represent different receivables. April sales collected in May are May cash inflows, not a duplication of April cash inflows. The standard cash collection formula (Month T collections = Rate_current × Month_T_sales + Rate_lag × Month_{T-1}_sales) does not double-count; it allocates each month's sales across two collection periods. A candidate selecting this option may have confused the allocation of receivables across periods with double-counting.",
    "ExplanationWrongD": "Option D claims May collections should exclude April sales collected in May. This fundamentally misunderstands the cash budget. The cash budget's purpose is to forecast when cash will be received, not when the sale was made. April sales collected in May are cash inflows in May — excluding them would understate May's cash position and potentially cause unnecessary borrowing. The standard methodology (including prior-month collections) is correct. A candidate selecting this option may have confused accrual accounting (recognize revenue when earned) with cash budgeting (recognize cash when received).",
