    "CorrectChoice": "C",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "Customer profitability ratio = (Customer Revenue - COGS - Customer-Specific Service Costs - Returns) / Customer Revenue. For each customer: Step 1 — Gross profit (Revenue - COGS). Nova: $290,000 - $174,000 = $116,000. Apex: $440,000 - $286,000 = $154,000. Crest: $215,000 - $118,250 = $96,750. Step 2 — Customer-specific costs. Nova: Delivery 42 x $72 = $3,024; Processing 258 x $38 = $9,804; Sales 16 x $105 = $1,680; Returns $4,200; Total = $18,708. Apex: Delivery 30 x $72 = $2,160; Processing 374 x $38 = $14,212; Sales 22 x $105 = $2,310; Returns $12,000; Total = $30,682. Crest: Delivery 22 x $72 = $1,584; Processing 168 x $38 = $6,384; Sales 10 x $105 = $1,050; Returns $1,800; Total = $10,818. Step 3 — Customer-level profit and ratio. Nova: $116,000 - $18,708 = $97,292; Ratio = 33.5%. Apex: $154,000 - $30,682 = $123,318; Ratio = 28.0%. Crest: $96,750 - $10,818 = $85,932; Ratio = 40.0%. The ranking by profitability ratio is: Crest (40.0%), Nova (33.5%), Apex (28.0%). Despite having the lowest revenue, Crest has the highest profitability ratio because its gross margin (55%) is the strongest, and its customer-specific costs (only $10,818) consume a much smaller share of gross profit than at Apex or Nova. This illustrates the core lesson of customer profitability analysis: high revenue does not equal high relative profitability.",
