    "ExplanationCorrect": "The project ROI = $54,000 / $300,000 = 18%. Since 18% < 22% (the division's current ROI), accepting the project would dilute the division's ROI to ($440,000 + $54,000) / ($2,000,000 + $300,000) = $494,000 / $2,300,000 = 21.48%. Because the manager's bonus is tied exclusively to divisional ROI, the manager has a personal incentive to reject the project (to preserve the higher 22% ROI and maximize the bonus). However, from the company's perspective, the project should be accepted because 18% > 14% WACC, meaning the project creates shareholder value. This is a classic goal congruence failure under ROI-based performance measurement: the manager's individually rational decision (reject) conflicts with the company's best interest (accept). The residual income (RI) method would avoid this conflict because RI = Operating Income - (Invested Capital x WACC). The project's RI = $54,000 - ($300,000 x 14%) = $54,000 - $42,000 = $12,000 positive, which would incentivize the manager to accept under an RI-based bonus.",
