    "ExplanationCorrect": "Common-size analysis expresses each income statement line item as a percentage of revenue, enabling the analyst to identify structural shifts in cost relationships that absolute-dollar comparisons may obscure. Computing the common-size percentages: 2023 — COGS $580,000 / $1,000,000 = 58.0%, SG&A $300,000 / $1,000,000 = 30.0%, Operating income $120,000 / $1,000,000 = 12.0%. 2024 — COGS $694,400 / $1,120,000 = 62.0%, SG&A $324,800 / $1,120,000 = 29.0%, Operating income $100,800 / $1,120,000 = 9.0%. The common-size analysis reveals that COGS expanded by 4.0 percentage points (58.0% to 62.0%) while SG&A actually improved by 1.0 percentage point (30.0% to 29.0%). Of the $120,000 revenue increase, COGS consumed $114,400 (95.3%), leaving only $5,600 in incremental contribution to cover SG&A growth. The margin compression from 12.0% to 9.0% (-3.0pp) decomposes as: +4.0pp from COGS deterioration minus 1.0pp from SG&A improvement = -3.0pp net. COGS is clearly the dominant factor. An exam trap is to focus on absolute-dollar changes in SG&A ($24,800 increase) while overlooking the common-size ratios that show COGS as the real margin driver.",
