    "ItemType": "MCQ",
    "ItemStyle": "single-select",
    "Stem": "Westgate Division reports net operating profit after tax (NOPAT) of $448,000, invested capital of $3,200,000, and a weighted average cost of capital (WACC) of 11%. The corporate controller is evaluating the division using economic value added (EVA). What is the division's EVA, and does it create shareholder value?",
    "Choices": {
      "A": "EVA = $96,000 positive; the division creates shareholder value because NOPAT of $448,000 exceeds the dollar cost of the capital employed ($3,200,000 x 11% = $352,000) by $96,000.",
      "B": "EVA = $49,280 after adjusting the WACC to an after-tax equivalent rate of 7.7%, computed as $448,000 - ($3,200,000 x 7.7%) = $201,600; the division creates substantial shareholder value.",
      "C": "EVA = -$96,000 negative; the division destroys shareholder value because the capital charge of $352,000 exceeds the NOPAT of $448,000.",
      "D": "EVA cannot be determined from the information provided because EVA requires adjustments for non-operating assets, the division's effective tax rate, and the market value of invested capital, none of which are disclosed."
    },
    "CorrectChoice": "A",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "Economic Value Added (EVA) measures whether a business unit generates returns above its cost of capital in dollar terms. The formula is: EVA = NOPAT - (Invested Capital x WACC). Substituting: EVA = $448,000 - ($3,200,000 x 0.11) = $448,000 - $352,000 = $96,000. Since EVA is positive, Westgate Division creates shareholder value — it generates $96,000 more in after-tax operating profit than the dollar cost of the capital it employs. This is the central insight of value-based management: a division can report positive accounting income (and even a healthy ROI of 14%, calculated as $448,000 / $3,200,000 = 14%) while destroying value if the return is below WACC, or it can create value when returns exceed the cost of capital, as Westgate does here. A common exam trap is to confuse ROI (14%) with EVA. ROI exceeding WACC (14% > 11%) is directionally consistent with positive EVA, but EVA measures the dollar magnitude of value creation — a high-ROI but small division may create less absolute value than a lower-ROI but larger division.",
