    "ItemType": "MCQ",
    "ItemStyle": "single-select",
    "Stem": "Clearwater Industries, a $780M industrial equipment distributor, implemented an aggressive sales incentive plan where regional sales directors receive bonuses equal to 50% of their base salary for exceeding quarterly revenue targets by 10%. The plan has no clawback provision, no quality review of booked revenue, and no cap on bonus amounts. An internal review finds that three regional directors systematically pressured customers to accept early shipments in the last week of each quarter (channel stuffing) and offered undisclosed side agreements allowing full returns. This resulted in $2.6M of overstated revenue over two quarters. Which best evaluates whether the performance measurement system aligns with COSO Principle 5?",
    "Choices": {
      "A": "The sales incentive plan violates COSO Principle 5 because it holds individuals accountable for performance targets without establishing complementary control activities — such as revenue recognition reviews, clawback provisions, and bonus caps — to mitigate the risk that aggressive targets will induce control overrides or fraudulent reporting.",
      "B": "The sales incentive plan is compliant with COSO Principle 5 because it clearly defines performance expectations and ties compensation to measurable outcomes, which is precisely what accountability requires.",
      "C": "The sales incentive plan fails under COSO Principle 5 because regional sales directors are operating-level employees who should not be subject to individual performance measures that create compensation variability.",
      "D": "The plan fails under COSO Principle 5 because bonuses should be capped at a maximum of 25% of base salary to prevent excessive risk-taking, regardless of whether complementary controls exist."
    },
    "CorrectChoice": "A",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "COSO Principle 5 states that the organization holds individuals accountable for their internal control responsibilities in the pursuit of objectives. Accountability is not simply about setting targets and paying for results — it requires establishing the full governance framework within which performance is evaluated: clear expectations, appropriate authority, complementary control activities, and consequences for control failures. Clearwater's sales incentive plan misses the critical second half of the accountability equation. The plan creates powerful financial incentives (50% of base salary, uncapped) to meet quarterly revenue targets, but it provides no guardrails: no revenue recognition review to verify that booked sales meet ASC 606 criteria, no clawback provision to recover bonuses paid on subsequently reversed revenue, and no cap to limit the magnitude of the incentive to override controls. A COSO Principle 5-compliant system would maintain the performance targets (accountability for results) while adding: independent revenue recognition review prior to bonus calculation, clawback provisions for restated revenue, caps on bonus multiples, and a control environment that disciplines control overrides regardless of revenue outcomes.",
