    "ExplanationCorrect": "COSO Principle 16 requires the organization to select, develop, and perform ongoing and/or separate evaluations to ascertain whether the components of internal control are present and functioning. The critical operational principle is proportionality: the frequency, scope, and depth of monitoring must be calibrated to the severity and velocity of the risks being monitored. Westgate's automated claims adjudication system processes high-dollar transactions at extremely high volume (12,000 per month, 144,000 per year) with a known vulnerability — rule #37's 72-hour gap — that can be exploited continuously and cumulatively. Annual point-in-time testing is fundamentally mismatched to this risk profile. The $1.7M loss accumulated over 11 months demonstrates that the monitoring interval exceeds the risk materialization interval by approximately 11:1. Appropriate monitoring for this environment would include: continuous automated monitoring of duplicate-claim patterns, exception reports generated daily or weekly, periodic data analytics looking for statistical anomalies, and more frequent separate evaluations (e.g., quarterly rather than annual).",
