    "ExplanationWrongA": "Option A incorrectly treats the board's approval as self-validating evidence that the risk appetite was satisfied. Under COSO ERM, the board's role is to evaluate proposed strategies against the stated risk appetite — its approval does not retroactively validate that a transaction is within appetite. If board approval alone were sufficient, the risk appetite statement would serve no constraining function. The statement's prohibition on risk-taking in areas where Apex lacks technical expertise is a specific, verifiable condition — not a subjective judgment that the board can reinterpret at will. A candidate selecting this option may be confusing the board's authority to set risk appetite with its obligation to apply that appetite consistently.",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "Option C incorrectly asserts that COSO ERM requires risk appetite to be stated in quantitative thresholds. COSO ERM recognizes that risk appetite can be expressed both qualitatively and quantitatively, depending on the nature of the risks and the organization's maturity. The statement 'activities where Apex possesses the technical expertise and operational capacity' is qualitative but entirely verifiable — Apex objectively had no satellite manufacturing expertise. The deficiency was not that the appetite statement was too subjective but that the board ignored its own qualitative guardrails. A candidate selecting this option may be overstating the precision requirements of risk appetite statements without recognizing that qualitative constraints are valid and enforceable governance tools.",
    "ExplanationWrongD": "Option D treats projected returns as sufficient justification for risk-taking regardless of the stated appetite. COSO ERM requires alignment between strategy and risk appetite, not merely between projected returns and risk exposure. The risk appetite statement explicitly prohibited risk-taking in areas where the company lacked technical expertise — the projected synergies do not remedy the absence of satellite manufacturing expertise. The board could have updated the risk appetite statement before approving the acquisition, but proceeding under an appetite that the transaction clearly violated represents a governance failure. A candidate selecting this option may be confusing investment evaluation (NPV-positive transactions) with risk governance (transactions consistent with stated boundaries of acceptable risk).",
