    "ExplanationWrongA": "Option A incorrectly concludes that the clawback alone satisfies Principle 1. While disciplinary action is an important component of demonstrating commitment to integrity, COSO Principle 1 requires an ongoing, comprehensive demonstration of ethical values that addresses the root causes of control failures. The board must evaluate whether the organizational culture, incentive design, and leadership behaviors that contributed to the misreporting have been substantively addressed. Treating the clawback as a complete and sufficient response — without cultural assessment, incentive redesign, or accountability for the systemic conditions that enabled the restatement — reduces Principle 1 to a checklist item rather than the foundational element of the control environment that the COSO framework intends.",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "Option C incorrectly separates executive compensation from the scope of internal control. COSO Principle 1 explicitly places responsibility for tone at the top with the board of directors and senior management. Compensation design — particularly performance-based bonuses tied to financial metrics — directly influences management behavior throughout the organization and is a core component of the control environment. When compensation structures create incentives that may encourage aggressive accounting or misreporting, the board's oversight of those structures and their consequences falls squarely within the scope of Principle 1. Dismissing the clawback as a matter for the compensation committee that is unrelated to internal control reflects a misunderstanding of the COSO framework.",
