    "Stem": "HarborPay, a payment processing company, relies on CloudCore, a third-party data center provider, for hosting its transaction processing systems. HarborPay's vendor management policy requires annual review of SOC reports for all critical vendors. The procurement manager, Lisa Tran, obtained CloudCore's SOC 1 Type II report, confirmed that it covered the relevant period, noted that the auditor issued an unmodified opinion, and filed it as evidence of vendor control effectiveness. Six months later, a data breach originating from CloudCore's infrastructure exposed 340,000 HarborPay customer records. The post-incident investigation revealed that the SOC 1 report: (1) covered only controls relevant to financial reporting (not operational security); (2) excluded the physical data center where HarborPay's servers were actually hosted (a colocation facility subcontracted by CloudCore); and (3) included a complementary user entity control consideration stating that HarborPay was responsible for monitoring logical access to its own applications hosted on CloudCore's infrastructure — a control HarborPay had not implemented. Under COSO Principle 15, which of the following best evaluates HarborPay's reliance on the SOC report?",
