    "CorrectChoice": "B",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "The Three Lines of Defense model requires not only that each line perform its assigned role but that the lines coordinate to provide integrated assurance. Pacific West's failure was systemic: the First Line approved exceptions without scrutiny, the Second Line stopped monitoring when the risk analyst position went vacant, and the Third Line deprioritized the trading desk in its audit plan. But the root cause is deeper than any single line's failure — it is the absence of a coordination mechanism that would have revealed the cumulative risk. When the First Line approved 23 of 27 exceptions, the Second Line should have detected a pattern; when the Second Line's monitoring ceased, the Third Line should have been aware of the coverage gap; when the Third Line deprioritized the trading desk, the board should have been informed that a critical business unit lacked assurance coverage. None of these cross-line signals occurred because there was no formal process for information sharing among the lines. The Three Lines model is designed to provide layered defense — each line compensates for the others' limitations — but layering only works when the lines are aware of each other's activities and gaps. The business interpretation is that organizations need an assurance map that identifies which risks are covered by which lines and surfaces coverage gaps for governance attention.",
