    "ExplanationCorrect": "COSO Principle 10 requires that control activities be deployed through policies and procedures that are proportionate to the assessed risk. The principle does not mandate a specific organizational structure or headcount — it requires that the residual risk (after controls are applied) be reduced to an acceptable level. For small entities, where hiring additional staff to achieve theoretical segregation of duties may be cost-prohibitive, COSO recognizes that compensating controls — particularly management oversight controls — can achieve the same control objective. The controller performing an independent review of all material journal entries and reconciliations is a management review control that mitigates the self-review threat: the senior accountant still prepares entries and reconciliations, but an independent reviewer (the controller) evaluates the output for reasonableness and accuracy. This approach achieves the control objective (independent verification of journal entries) without the incremental cost of a fifth employee. The business interpretation is that COSO is principles-based, not rules-based — it expects organizations to design control activities appropriate to their size, complexity, and resources, not to conform to an idealized organizational chart.",
